      <section class="examples-section kst-brief" id="kst-basics" aria-labelledby="kst-basics-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">Korea uses one civil clock</p>
        <h2 id="kst-basics-title">What is Korea Standard Time?</h2>
        <p>Korea Standard Time is the official time used throughout South Korea. Seoul, Busan, Incheon, Daegu, Daejeon, Gwangju, Ulsan, Jeju, and Korean manufacturing regions all share the same UTC+9 clock. That uniformity is helpful for domestic planning: a client call in Seoul and a factory inspection in Busan do not need a local-zone adjustment.</p>
        <p>International planning is where the work begins. A Korean business morning is frequently the previous evening in Eastern North America. A late Korean meeting can become a useful Eastern morning. A mid-afternoon Korean deadline may sit near midnight in New York. A strong KST EST Converter should therefore expose the weekday, date, offset, and Eastern daylight-saving label instead of only adding or subtracting a number.</p>
        <figure class="kst-wide-figure reveal">
