            <span>Korea Standard Time</span>
            <strong>UTC+9</strong>
            <p>No current daylight-saving adjustment in South Korea.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>Eastern Standard Time</span>
            <strong>UTC-5</strong>
            <p>KST is 14 hours ahead when Eastern locations are on EST.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>Eastern Daylight Time</span>
            <strong>UTC-4</strong>
            <p>KST is 13 hours ahead when Eastern locations are on EDT.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p>The phrase <strong>Korea Standard Time to Eastern Standard Time</strong> is useful as a search term, but real calendars should use the active Eastern label. In winter, subtract 14 hours from a Seoul time to read New York time. During Eastern daylight saving time, subtract 13 hours. The Korean side does not cause the seasonal shift.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="examples-section" aria-labelledby="single-zone-title">
        <h2 id="single-zone-title">Why South Korea uses a single timezone</h2>
        <p>South Korea is geographically compact compared with countries that span several civil time zones. A single national clock keeps schools, broadcast schedules, public agencies, airlines, banks, hospitals, factories, and rail services aligned. For companies that manage multiple Korean offices, this removes a domestic scheduling layer and makes KST a stable planning reference.</p>
        <p>That stability does not remove the international date problem. Seoul can be in a new workday while Eastern North America is still finishing the prior evening. Korean client communications, manufacturing cutoffs, classroom sessions, online game servers, and stock-market notes should therefore include both the Korean date and the Eastern date when the two are different.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="examples-section" aria-labelledby="dst-title">
        <h2 id="dst-title">Does South Korea observe Daylight Saving Time?</h2>
        <p>South Korea does not currently observe daylight saving time. KST remains UTC+9 in January, April, July, October, and every other month. Eastern Time is the moving part: many Eastern locations switch to EDT in the spring and return to EST in the fall. That is why a Seoul-to-New-York meeting can be 14 hours apart during one part of the year and 13 hours apart during another.</p>
        <p>For recurring work, the risky weeks are the U.S. daylight-saving transition periods. A team that agreed to meet at 10 PM KST and 8 AM Eastern in winter may see that same Seoul time become 9 AM Eastern after the U.S. spring change. Reconfirm recurring calls, launch freezes, office hours, and production maintenance windows around those dates.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="examples-section" id="meeting-hours" aria-labelledby="meeting-hours-title">
        <h2 id="meeting-hours-title">Best meeting hours between Seoul and New York</h2>
        <p>The workday overlap is narrow because the regions are on opposite sides of the day. Seoul morning is usually Eastern evening. Seoul late evening is Eastern morning. For executive calls, client reviews, support escalations, and product approvals, the most humane compromise often sits around <strong>10 PM to 11 PM KST</strong>.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Korea Standard Time</th><th>During EST</th><th>During EDT</th><th>Planning note</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {quickRows.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{row.kst}</td>
